
  I recommend [1mES modules[0m: it gives you a genuine shared module for the future forms without adding a build system to a
  repo that has none, and the cost is one attribute plus serving over http instead of opening the file directly.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should the shared identity module be delivered to the browser? → ES modules (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last clarifying question before I put approaches together. You said "persist," which spans two quite different
  behaviors:

  - [1mAcross browser restarts ([38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m)[0m — the user's ID is still there tomorrow. This is what most people mean by
    persist, and it's what makes the ID available to future forms without re-login. The cost is on shared or public
    machines: the previous user's identity sits there until something explicitly clears it, so logout has to be a real
    code path that removes it, not just a navigation.
  - [1mPer tab, cleared on close ([38;5;153msessionStorage[39m)[0m — same API, scoped to the tab. Much safer default on shared machines and
    it self-cleans. But a new tab starts empty, so "works across the app" stops holding the moment someone opens a
    second tab.

  Both are one-line differences in the identity module and genuinely cheap to change later, since only that module
  touches storage.

  I recommend [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m, because "other forms will need it later" implies the ID has to outlive a single tab — paired
  with an explicit [38;5;153mclear()[39m in the identity module so logout actually removes it.
[38;5;246m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
